#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198084 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "1a2f8100-8f2e-5c6b-ade3-7192a914323f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13171" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n2. $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n3. $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\:: \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3$, and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4386/Source/Images/1312340fd7cc5c013bf4edfc5b131205.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is\n$x \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3$\nand can be expressed as $[MATH: x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).]$\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is\n$-3 \\leq x \\leq 3$\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3]$.\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1$. Therefore,\n$A = \\{\\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{3, 4, \\ldots \\}$.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2$. Therefore,\n$B = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}$.\nConsequently, $\\mid B \\mid = 7$, which is finite.\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2$. Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198085 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "3ccf363e-d311-50e9-83b1-929ef1148554" :question/math-academy-id "e-13174" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n2. $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\:: \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n3. $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\:: \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin (x\\pi) = 1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxπ &= \\frac{π}{2} + 2mπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} + 2m, m ∈ Z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z$. Therefore,\n$A = \\{\\}$.\nConsequently, $\\mid A \\mid = 0$, which is finite.\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m$, we have\n$B = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}$.\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C$, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198086 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/id #uuid "359028be-0c1d-5f5c-ace7-3d7aeb36d9e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-13175" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n2. $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n3. $C = \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$f^{(0)}(x)|= e^{5x}; f^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{5x}) = 5e^{5x}; f^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(5e^{5x}) = 25e^{5x}; f^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(25e^{5x}) = 125e^{5x}; ⋮$\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n$A = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}$\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$g^{(0)}(x)|= 6e^{-x}; g^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(6e^{-x}) =-6e^{-x}; g^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(-6e^{-x}) = 6e^{-x}; g^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(6e^{-x}) =-6e^{-x}; ⋮$\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n$B = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}$\nConsequently, $\\mid B \\mid = 2$, which is finite.\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$h^{(0)}(x)|= 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; ⋮$\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n$C = \\{2e^{x+3} \\}$\nConsequently, $\\mid C \\mid = 1$, which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb3bbee5-0ea0-57e9-9979-9fb6497efbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195266} {:answer/id #uuid "d585b964-7781-5f66-b454-f1b872b94f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195267} {:answer/id #uuid "22d011d0-78d4-5a16-a572-72ba36a62857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195268} {:answer/id #uuid "26cb01aa-c87a-59f2-bc07-ef883cadda07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195269} {:answer/id #uuid "596f09fb-51fa-5bd0-8c1e-8a80797a93bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22d011d0-78d4-5a16-a572-72ba36a62857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195268} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca482694-e753-5b94-a4be-f0e5e63f7310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195265}] :question/id #uuid "e9482778-6f45-5e93-913d-289acb526444" :question/math-academy-id "q-181536" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: n^{2} - 5n + 6 > 0}$\n2. $B = {m ∈ Z: m^{2} - 5m + 6 < 0}$\n3. $C = {q ∈ Q: q^{2} - 5q + 6 < 0}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8977de-4d1b-5a0f-abf2-f8970d0b056f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195278} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc5d032-c92d-5d49-891c-26a5377a22e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195279} {:answer/id #uuid "0a32bb9d-dc47-5a86-97c7-78c20c47e5c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195280} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea2f1f4-7e3e-5782-997a-e1e3de251b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195281} {:answer/id #uuid "71e74963-91d2-5eef-b367-b092a3424962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d8977de-4d1b-5a0f-abf2-f8970d0b056f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195278} :answer-field/id #uuid "45de3bee-dbad-5b10-affc-2a7a874ef8cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195277}] :question/id #uuid "4e83b937-f1ab-5064-ab2b-f81fab22743d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181597" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\sin (2nπ) = 1}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\sin (2qπ) = 1}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\sin (2xπ) = 1}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d43098da-8501-52f2-8ecd-616271ac75cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195284} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cb8316-aecd-5b74-a0b8-3e719fa59171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195285} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0734b-ee1c-57ce-b7f2-dacf18348b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.typ